<h2>6. Evaluation and Reflection</h2>

<h3>6.1 Proposal vs. Implementation Comparison</h3>
<p>Out of 17 proposed features, <b>14 were fully implemented</b>, 1 was partially implemented, and 2 remain unimplemented. The core delivery workflow, real-time features, and gamification system were all delivered as proposed. The main shortfall is the lack of an admin dashboard and the absence of CUET email domain restriction on registration.</p>

<p><i>[Insert screenshots of key working features here]</i></p>

<h3>6.2 Major Obstacles and Solutions</h3>

<p><b>Challenge 1: Supabase Auth Callback Deadlock</b></p>
<p>When <code>onAuthStateChange</code> fires the SIGNED_IN event, making Supabase calls within the callback caused deadlocks because the auth token refresh was still in progress.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b> Wrapped profile loading in <code>setTimeout(() => loadProfile(session.user.id), 0)</code> to escape the callback context before making further Supabase calls.</p>

<p><b>Challenge 2: Race Conditions on Request Accept</b></p>
<p>Two users could simultaneously try to accept the same open request, causing data corruption.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b> Used conditional updates with <code>.eq('status', 'Open')</code> in the Supabase query, ensuring only one user's update succeeds. The second user gets a null response and sees the request as already accepted.</p>

<p><b>Challenge 3: Real-time Message Ordering</b></p>
<p>Messages arriving via WebSocket could appear out of order when combined with the initial fetch.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b> Used a <code>useRef</code> to store the latest callback reference, and re-fetched the full message list on any real-time event rather than trying to merge individual messages, ensuring consistent ordering.</p>

<p><b>Challenge 4: snake_case vs camelCase Mismatch</b></p>
<p>Supabase returns PostgreSQL column names in snake_case, but the React frontend uses camelCase conventions. This caused numerous bugs when properties were undefined.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b> Created centralized mapper functions (<code>mapProfile</code>, <code>mapRequest</code>, <code>mapMessage</code>, <code>mapNotification</code>) in <code>helpers.js</code> and a <code>toSnakeCase</code> utility for updates. All data passes through these mappers.</p>

<p><b>Challenge 5: Atomic Stat Updates</b></p>
<p>Updating deliverer statistics (earnings, completion count, rating average) from the frontend was prone to race conditions when multiple deliveries completed simultaneously.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b> Moved stat updates to server-side PostgreSQL RPC functions with <code>SECURITY DEFINER</code> privilege. These functions use atomic <code>UPDATE...SET</code> operations that are guaranteed to be consistent.</p>

<h3>6.3 Performance Observations</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Metric</th><th>Observation</th><th>Optimization</th></tr>
<tr><td>Initial page load</td><td>~1.5s on 4G connection</td><td>Vite code splitting, lazy route loading</td></tr>
<tr><td>Supabase query latency</td><td>50-150ms per query</td><td>Pagination (30 items/page), selective column fetching</td></tr>
<tr><td>Real-time message delivery</td><td>&lt;200ms end-to-end</td><td>WebSocket keep-alive via Supabase SDK</td></tr>
<tr><td>Notification polling</td><td>Replaced with real-time</td><td>Switched from 5s polling to WebSocket subscription</td></tr>
<tr><td>Theme toggle</td><td>Instant (&lt;16ms)</td><td>CSS class toggle on &lt;html&gt; element, no re-render</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>6.4 What's Missing and Improvement Opportunities</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Student email verification:</b> Any Google account can register — CUET domain enforcement would prevent misuse.</li>
<li><b>Server-side permission guards:</b> Request status transitions are validated only in frontend code. Server-side RPC functions with validation would be more secure.</li>
<li><b>Rate limiting:</b> No protection against API abuse from malicious clients.</li>
<li><b>Image upload:</b> Requests cannot include item photos. Supabase Storage integration would solve this.</li>
<li><b>Admin dashboard:</b> No moderation interface exists for managing reported content or users.</li>
<li><b>Payment gateway:</b> Payment is tracked as a UI gesture, not through actual bKash/Nagad APIs.</li>
<li><b>Automated testing:</b> No unit or E2E test suite — all testing was manual.</li>
<li><b>PWA/Push notifications:</b> No service worker for offline support or mobile push.</li>
</ul>

<div class="pagebreak"></div>

<h2>7. Conclusion and Contributions</h2>

<h3>7.1 Course Learning Objectives Met</h3>
<p>This project demonstrates proficiency in the following course learning objectives:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Frontend development:</b> Built a complete SPA with React 19, demonstrating component architecture, hooks, context API, and client-side routing.</li>
<li><b>Backend integration:</b> Integrated with Supabase (PostgreSQL) for authentication, database CRUD, real-time subscriptions, and RPC functions.</li>
<li><b>Database design:</b> Designed a normalized relational schema with proper foreign keys, RLS policies, triggers, and stored procedures.</li>
<li><b>API design:</b> Used RESTful patterns via Supabase PostgREST with proper error handling and data mapping.</li>
<li><b>Authentication &amp; security:</b> Implemented OAuth 2.0 with JWT token management and Row Level Security.</li>
<li><b>Real-time features:</b> Built live chat and notifications using WebSocket technology.</li>
<li><b>Responsive design:</b> Mobile-first approach with Tailwind CSS, supporting all screen sizes.</li>
<li><b>Deployment:</b> Deployed to Vercel with environment variable management and production builds.</li>
</ul>

<h3>7.2 Contributions to Stakeholders</h3>
<p>CUETConnect directly addresses a real problem faced by CUET students. The platform provides:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>For requesters:</b> A reliable way to get items delivered without wasting hours traveling.</li>
<li><b>For deliverers:</b> An opportunity to earn money on trips they are already making.</li>
<li><b>For the campus community:</b> A trust-building platform with ratings, badges, and accountability.</li>
</ul>

<h3>7.3 Personal Technical Growth</h3>
<ul>
<li>Gained proficiency in React 19 with modern hooks and Context API patterns.</li>
<li>Learned Supabase as a full Backend-as-a-Service platform (Auth, DB, Realtime, RLS).</li>
<li>Understood Row Level Security design patterns for PostgreSQL.</li>
<li>Practiced real-time WebSocket integration for chat and notifications.</li>
<li>Built a complete gamification system with ratings, badges, and leaderboards.</li>
<li>Deployed a production application to Vercel with CI/CD.</li>
</ul>

<h3>7.4 Self-Evaluation</h3>
<p>The project successfully delivers a functional, polished, and feature-rich campus delivery platform. The core delivery workflow works end-to-end, real-time features provide an engaging user experience, and the gamification system encourages continued usage. The main areas for improvement are server-side permission hardening, CUET email enforcement, and automated testing. Overall, the project demonstrates strong frontend development skills, effective BaaS integration, and practical database design.</p>

